+  "bottleneck_statement": "In a robot-manipulation WAM that jointly denoises future observations and actions, a weight or activation perturbation can have a small local discrepancy yet change an action chunk, alter the next observation, and thereby change the inputs to later coupled predictions; the needed downstream consequence is not identified by current numerical calibration. QuantWM (arxiv:2602.02110v1) shows that iterative world-model inference can accumulate quantization error and decouple a planning objective from task success, but its DINO-WM setting is latent rollout/planning rather than a coupled video/action WAM. QuantWAMs (arxiv:2607.28405v1) extends PTQ to coupled WAMs with coordinate-aware activation calibration, joint video-action empirical-Fisher saliency, and fixed-intervention denoising-step auditing, yet explicitly uses its local discrepancy profile as a schedule-audit signal rather than as a marginal closed-loop task-gain estimate. The unresolved structural failure is therefore that precision decisions cannot yet produce a validated action-level consequence of numerical error across the coupled denoising and subsequent observation/action loop, allowing local calibration fidelity to remain decoupled from contact-critical control reliability.",
+  "closest_adjacent": [
+    {
+      "paper_id": "arxiv:2607.28405v1",
+      "is_anchor": true,
+      "summary_and_residue": "QuantWAMs calibrates a coupled WAM with coordinate-admissible activation pooling, joint video-action empirical-Fisher layer saliency, and replay of full-precision closed-loop snapshots to choose a fixed-count denoising-step protection set. Its method explicitly treats the replay profile as a schedule-audit signal rather than a marginal closed-loop task-gain estimate, and does not establish which local perturbation changes the final action chunk or later observations."
+    },
+    {
+      "paper_id": "arxiv:2602.02110v1",
+      "is_anchor": false,
+      "summary_and_residue": "QuantWM measures weight/activation PTQ, bit width, granularity, and long-horizon effects in DINO-WM, finding error accumulation and a mismatch between planning loss and task success at aggressive precision. It does not study coupled video/action WAM denoising or determine how a numerical perturbation in a WAM branch changes action generation and subsequent observations."
+    },
+    {
+      "paper_id": "semanticscholar:b5be12ccbb037be57a1dbc03fbd43d379170055f",
+      "is_anchor": false,
+      "summary_and_residue": "Fast-WAM separates video co-training from explicit future generation and shows that video supervision can remain useful when test-time future synthesis is removed. Its controlled study omits numerical weight/activation quantization and the outer autoregressive loop, so it does not determine how low-bit perturbations affect a coupled future/action denoising loop."
+    }
+  ],
+  "what_phase_0_did_not_address": [
+    "additive: No retrieved WAM-quantization leaf establishes the downstream consequence of a numerical perturbation at a module or denoising step for the final action chunk and the next closed-loop observation in contact-critical manipulation; this is the unresolved residue of QuantWAMs (arxiv:2607.28405v1), not a request for a particular remedy. — stakes: practitioners may deploy a memory- and latency-efficient WAM that preserves local calibration metrics but fails on contact-sensitive actions, while researchers may mistake local fidelity for transferable closed-loop reliability.",
+    "subtractive: No retrieved WAM-quantization leaf tests whether the local numerical-fidelity surrogate inherited from layer-local PTQ is actually sufficient for precision selection in the coupled video/action setting, rather than an assumed proxy for downstream task impact; this questions the inherited assumption and leaves the replacement open. — stakes: precision-allocation conclusions can rest on an unexamined surrogate and invalidate comparisons across WAMs or tasks, while deployment teams cannot tell whether an apparent speed or memory gain preserves the action behavior they require."
